[TEXT]
MID: 5ec4897eb37fb91bf4e32925a0c71b45
STATEMENT: >>>
[LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-DEPLOYMENT-LIFECYCLE-unified-cloudflare-management, 単一 Cloudflare Tunnel と製品公開分離] が管理経路と公開ゲートの契約を所有する。正式運用には統合実サービス受入を要する。

> On a pristine approved VM, the browser enrollment flow uses a one-time setup key and a restricted Fastify API behind Cloudflare. The protected production workflow uses the normal same-origin frontend. See [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-AUTH-RUNTIME-first-owner-through-the-browser, first owner through the browser] and the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-INITIAL-DEPLOYMENT-FLOW, initial deployment design].

The [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-INITIAL-DEPLOYMENT-FLOW, initial deployment state machine] and [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-UPDATE-FLOW, compatible update/rollback state machine] are normative. The implemented `bootstrap` stage converges approved host, secrets, base, and auth-runtime tasks; owner enrollment and protected application activation remain separate.

The protected GitHub Actions workflow reaches the pinned SSH host through Cloudflare Access Service Auth and Tunnel. Ansible converges the rootless PostgreSQL and API Quadlets and controls the independent host publication gate. `site.yml` rejects the obsolete pull-updater stages.

The protected controller generates its inventory and pins SSH. An isolated stage test can use its own inventory:

```bash
ansible-playbook -i /private/codexia-inventory.yml deploy/ansible/site.yml -e codexia_stage=inspect
```

Supported stages are `inspect`, `bootstrap`, `host`, `secrets`, `base`, `auth-runtime`, `deploy`, `deploy-lifecycle-fixture` and `restore-drill`. The lifecycle fixture stage runs only from the run-bound disposable GitHub Actions workflow; it is not a production operation.

The `host` stage validates Ubuntu 26.04, systemd and cgroup v2, and creates or adopts the authorized service account. The `secrets` stage installs the two PostgreSQL credentials without implicit rotation. The `base` stage converges networks, volumes and PostgreSQL 17, verifies the existing host connector, and installs the initial closed publication gate. These stages do not publish new API images.

The `deploy` stage requires a certified immutable Full image, main-source provenance certificate, and a compatible deployed UI epoch. It recovers interrupted transactions, serializes application changes, and observes candidate health. Before replacing an existing API it requires off-VM backup through the active Console revision and VM-local executor settings. Image-only updates refuse changed migration fingerprint, persistence format or API epoch. A failed candidate restores the prior Quadlet and release record.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
MID: 1df14330a3ff1345aee8ee7d88cd3955
UID: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT-bring-up-a-new-vm
TITLE: Bring up a new VM

[TEXT]
MID: 897ea1be07456f0b2531e9899cecc169
STATEMENT: >>>
Start [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PRODUCTION-BOOTSTRAP, Protected production Bootstrap] with the pinned administrator login and approved target. The controller derives the deployment public key in the runtime Environment, generates the Ansible inventory, and installs the key with OpenSSH's `restrict` option. On an approved fresh VM the rootless service generates its DB and authentication credentials. R4 encrypts these values for independent recovery before provisioning the DB. The [example inventory](https://github.com/yohn-jp/codexia/blob/main/deploy/ansible/inventory.example.yml) describes the individual stages used by disposable hosts and explicit recovery operations.

A fresh R4 ends with the DB and authentication schema prepared. R5 opens the restricted API for the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-AUTH-RUNTIME-first-owner-through-the-browser, browser handoff] after the certified frontend is available.

After enrollment and preparation, the protected production workflow can activate the certified image with the two first-install approvals and handle subsequent compatible updates. See the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT-first-application-activation, first activation procedure].

Schema and persistence-format changes are not currently supported by the image-only push workflow. No startup migration bypass is permitted. The candidate remains blocked until an independent migration procedure defines approval, backup, execution, verification, and tested restore.

The host lock and deployment state are under `~/.local/state/codexia-deploy`. The backup executable is `deploy/backup/backup.sh`. The `restore-drill` stage is executed on an isolated local recovery host and never restores production data. See [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-PUSH-DEPLOYMENT, Push deployment] and [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP, Backup].

No polling, timer, signed channel, route selector or compatibility controller is installed. Repository changes do not modify the live VM.
<<<

